{"id": "22028989-8a52-425e-8482-6e0e9ce21fbf", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:27:31.325117", "source": "user", "parent_id": "0379c5d8-e47e-4093-8da3-29c89fffa0a0", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is a design-only task (see track.md). We must produce a final experiment *design* — with rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility — for the question: *How does the runtime clipping ratio affect perplexity on LLAMA2-7B under symmetric input quantization and asymmetric KV-cache quantization, and what fixed clipping ratio best balances quantization error and accuracy?* Do NOT run experiments; do NOT fabricate results. Network and GPU are disabled in the runtime bubble, so treat any execution as out of scope and focus on the design deliverable only. Fixed requirements already locked in: (a) model = LLAMA2-7B; (b) activation/input quantization = symmetric; (c) KV-cache quantization = asymmetric; (d) primary metric = perplexity (PPL); (e) independent variable = clipping ratio (a scalar applied during runtime quantization). For this initial inspection turn, ONLY provide: 1) **Repository / input inspection** — list the contents of /workspace, summarize what (if anything) in the workspace is relevant (any starter code, configs, eval scripts, weight pointers, README). If the workspace is empty or irrelevant, say so. Do not assume external datasets or model weights are available. 2) **Proposed approach** — outline how you would structure the experiment design: (i) the sweep over clipping ratios (range, granularity, grid vs. log-spacing, and a justification), (ii) how 'clipping ratio' will be defined operationally for symmetric input quantization (e.g., fraction of max |x| used as clip threshold, with and without per-token/per-channel/per-tensor scope — pick one and justify), (iii) how asymmetric KV-cache quantization will be specified (min/max calibration, granularity such as per-head/per-token/per-channel, dtype equivalent like int8), (iv) evaluation protocol (which perplexity benchmark(s) — wikitext-2, c4, ptb — and why; sequence length, stride, calibration data, number of samples; baseline configurations such as FP16, no-clip, and a reference adaptive method like percentile/batch-stat calibration), (v) controls and ablations to isolate the effect of the clipping ratio from effects of granularity/calibration, (vi) the analysis plan (PPL vs. ratio curve, error decomposition into clipping vs. rounding, sensitivity checks), and (vii) feasibility (compute/memory estimate, what would be required if execution were in scope, and what remains viable purely on paper). 3) **Consequential assumptions and uncertainties** — explicitly enumerate: which design choices you are assuming because the brief does not specify them (e.g., evaluation dataset, context length, granularity of activation quantization, KV-cache group size, calibration strategy, sweep bounds), and which uncertainties could materially change the recommended optimal-ratio conclusion. End your turn after these three items. Do not produce the final experiment design yet — we will discuss the approach before authorizing the full design write-up.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How does the clipping ratio used during runtime quantization affect perplexity on LLAMA2-7B when applying symmetric input quantization and asymmetric KV cache quantization? What is the optimal fixed clipping ratio that balances quantization error and model accuracy in inference?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [\"neurips2024/94328/2:setup_1\", \"neurips2024/94328/2:setup_10\", \"neurips2024/94328/2:setup_17\"], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
